  "contract": "A '+' in the input is the caller stating the number is already international, and the digits that follow it must be the country code. THREE rules deliberately diverge from the Tracive app's normalizer, and they are not the same kind of thing:\n1. A dial code that disagrees with the country named is refused. Every input this rejects produced an unusable key in the app too — safe.\n2. Emptiness is re-checked AFTER the trunk prefix is stripped, so '000', '62' and '1' are refused. The app folds these to '+62' and '+1', which cannot match any record — safe, same as (1).\n3. '+00<dial>' is refused. The app DOES accept this form and folds it correctly, so here the SDKs are stricter than the app on an input the app handles. That is the intended trade: what follows '+' must be a country code, '00' is none, and admitting one malformed concatenation has no principled stopping point ('+000062', '++62').\n4. A number that is nothing but zeros is refused. Most countries' trunk rule strips the zeros and rule 2 catches it, but Italy, San Marino, North America and Russia keep leading zeros, so '+390000000' used to survive, match E.164, reach the network and be billed for a miss. The app folds it the same way, so this is a safe divergence like 1 and 2 — no number that works today folds differently."
}
